📊 Statistical Analysis of Poverty in West Java, 2021
Overview:
This project presents a statistical analysis of poverty data across regencies and cities in West Java Province in 2021 using official data published by Statistics Indonesia (BPS). The analysis aims to examine poverty conditions across regions, measure the number of poor residents, calculate poverty proportions, and identify variations in poverty levels through descriptive statistical methods.
The project provides insights into regional socioeconomic disparities and demonstrates how statistical techniques can be applied to analyze public data effectively.

Objectives:
Analyze poverty conditions across regencies and cities in West Java.
Measure the distribution of poor populations among regions.
Calculate descriptive statistical measures such as mean and variance.
Examine regional disparities in poverty levels.
Interpret statistical findings to support socioeconomic understanding.

Dataset:
Source: Statistics Indonesia (BPS) – West Java Province, 2021

Dataset Variables:
Regency/City Name
Population Size
Poverty Percentage
Number of Poor Residents (Thousands)
Poverty Proportion
Mean Deviation (xi − x̄)
Squared Deviation (xi − x̄)²

Analysis Performed:
Data Cleaning and Preparation
Poverty Population Analysis
Poverty Percentage Analysis
Proportion Analysis
Mean Calculation
Variance (S²) Calculation
Poverty Distribution Analysis
Statistical Interpretation
Data Visualization using Microsoft Excel

Key Findings:
The average number of poor residents in West Java in 2021 was 14.61 thousand people.
The variance (S²) was 120.36, indicating considerable variation among regions.
Bogor Regency recorded the highest number of poor residents with 39.94 thousand people.
Banjar City recorded the lowest number of poor residents with 0.95 thousand people.
The results highlight significant socioeconomic differences across regencies and cities in West Java.

